it's been another tragic week for US  military aviation five service members  have died and the accidents are  continuing national security  correspondent Jennifer Griffin is at the  Pentagon with the fallout could even  Jennifer  good evening Brett late today the Air  Force identified the pilot killed in  yesterday's f-16 crash outside Las Vegas  Maj Steven del baño flew for the elites  Thunderbirds had more than 3500 flying  hours in 30 different types of planes he  was very experienced yet his fighter jet  crashed during a routine training flight  near Nellis Air Force Base it marks the  third crash for the Thunderbirds in less  than two years and the third military  aircraft to crash in the past two days  on Tuesday a Marine Corps Super Stallion  helicopter crashed in southern  California killing four Marines earlier  that day in East in the East African  nation of Djibouti a marine Harrier jet  crashed on takeoff the pilot ejected and  was released from the hospital but today  the Djibouti government grounded all US  military flights including drones from  the largest American base in Africa  hindering counterterrorism operations  there have been five non combat aviation  crashes this year alone killing nine  service members thirty-seven were killed  in air crashes last year nearly double  the number killed in 2016 but a top  general at the Pentagon would not call  it a crisis I would reject wave in  crisis  those are mishaps that occurred we're  gonna look at each one in turn each one  is tragic we regret each one we'll look  at them carefully I'm certainly not  prepared to say that it's a wave of  mishaps or of some form of crisis the  House Armed Services Chairman Mac  Thornberry warned the White House not to  rob the military's readiness money to  fund the National Guard at the border  when President George W Bush sent six  thousand troops to the Mexico border it  cost four hundred and fifteen million  dollars money that was stripped from the  Air Force and Navy's operations and  maintenance budgets the Pentagon just  received 700 billion dollars a sizeable  portion of that is supposed to help fix  aircraft and improve pilot training read  Jennifer Griffin at the Pentagon  Jennifer thank you
 

